// @emit-error emitProgram: ref valtype with no interned shape
// A `for-in` over an UNDECLARED list whose element has a nested object field matching a
// DECLARED union arm's layout — silent-class-inventory D179, and the only `compiler trap`
// the census grid ever produced from source.
//
// `Cir2` is a union member, so `collectS` gives it no `sNames` row and nothing interns the
// nested `{ c2: 1 }` shape. No ref-list slot is minted for `c`, and `refListSlotOfExpr`
// does NOT answer -1 on that miss — it CLAMPS to `0`. With no ref list interned anywhere
// in the module `rlElemKindTbl` is EMPTY, so `forInElemKind`'s `rlElemKindTbl[rslot]` read
// was an out-of-bounds array access INSIDE the compiler: `vl check` rc 0, then
// `wasm trap: out of bounds array access` and `vl build -o` writing no module at all.
//
// THE `is` IS NOT LOAD-BEARING — deleting the whole `if` line and leaving `hit = 7` traps
// identically on the pre-fix compiler. Two other one-line ablations both RUN: delete the
// three `type` lines, or declare `Cir2` without making it a union member. What the trap
// needs is the declared-arm layout and an otherwise empty ref-list table; intern any second
// ref list and index 0 is in bounds, the ladder proceeds, and this same loud reject is what
// comes out.
//
// The message is the one the INDEX spelling of this program (`c[0].r is Cir2`) has always
// given — `fbValtype`'s bound guard recorded it in an earlier section, and `emitFail`
// records without halting, so the trap downstream was all that kept it from being printed.
type Cir2 = { c2: i32 }
type Sq2 = { s2: i32 }
type Shape2 = Cir2 | Sq2
const c = [{ r: { c2: 1 } }]
let hit = 0
for zz in c {
  if zz.r is Cir2 { hit = 7 }
}
print(hit)
